{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>I will certainly return to you at the appointed time, alive</b>. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 18:10): the angel did not announce that he himself would return to him, but rather he spoke on the mission of HaMakom, as in \"And the angel of YHWH said to her, 'I will greatly multiply your seed'\" (Genesis 16:10) — and it is not in his power to multiply, but he spoke on the mission of HaMakom. So too here, he spoke to him on the mission of HaMakom. And the Rav needed to say this because here HaKadosh Barukh Hu said to him, 'At the appointed time I will return to you,' yet whether it was the angel or HaKadosh Barukh Hu, we do not find that He returned to him at his appointed time. Perhaps it is included in the expression \"And YHWH remembered Sarah as He had said, and YHWH did for Sarah as He had spoken\" (Genesis 21:1). And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 21:2) that \"And YHWH said to Abraham\" is the statement of the angel speaking in the name of his sender, and he did return to him at the appointed time when he had spoken with him, even though it was not written. And the correct interpretation in my eyes is that it is from the expression \"at the return of the year\" (2 Samuel 11:1) — he says: I will certainly return to you at this same season when you will be alive, and there will be a son to Sarah your wife, and this is as was said to Abraham (Genesis 17:21), \"At this appointed time next year,\" and \"I will return\" is like \"And YHWH your Elohim will return and gather you\" (Deuteronomy 30:3)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The Rav refers to Maimonides. The final quotation from Deuteronomy reads וְשָׁב in the ms but the verse has וְשָׁב; Ramban cites it with doubled verb as in the Torah text; rendered literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:31:04.451517894Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}